\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 letter-salutation\cf4 ">Dear Parents and Guidance Counselors,</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 letter-text\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >This week, the week of March 23\'9627, 2026, is one of the most consequential in the annual higher education calendar \'97 and this year it carries more institutional weight than usual. Ivy Day fell on Thursday, March 26. The Class of 2030 decisions from all eight Ivy League universities were released simultaneously at 7:00 p.m. Eastern. For thousands of families, this was the culmination of years of preparation. I want to address that moment directly.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >At the same time, three other stories this week demand serious attention from every family navigating college planning \'97 not just those with Ivy League targets. The Trump administration transferred management of the federal student loan portfolio to the Treasury Department, a historic restructuring of how America's $1.7 trillion student debt load will be administered. Congress is confronting a looming $5.5 billion Pell Grant shortfall that could reduce aid for over seven million students. And new data reveals that the NIH \'97 the engine of university research \'97 has distributed only 15% of its annual budget at the fiscal year's midpoint, threatening lab closures, career disruptions, and the very infrastructure that makes elite research universities distinctive.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >Each of these stories has a specific, actionable implication for families. Ivy Day is the most emotionally immediate; the others are more structurally important over the long term. Both matter. Here is what you need to know.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 letter-sig\cf4 ">\'97 Dr. Nathan Hurwitz</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

