\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 article\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-section\cf4 " id="\cf6 t01\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-number-row\cf4 "><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-num\cf4 ">TOPIC 01</\cf5 span\cf4 ><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-cat\cf4 ">Admissions / Ivy Day 2026</\cf5 span\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 h2\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-title\cf4 ">Ivy Day 2026: Class of 2030 Decisions Released \'97 What the Numbers Tell Us and What Families Do Next</\cf5 h2\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-deck\cf4 ">All eight Ivy League universities released Regular Decision results for the Class of 2030 on Thursday, March 26, 2026 at 7:00 p.m. Eastern. Acceptance rates remain in the low single digits. Here is how families should process and respond to every possible outcome.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-rule\cf4 "></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-grid\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-main\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-body\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >Ivy Day 2026 arrived on Thursday, March 26 \'97 the most emotionally charged moment in the annual college admissions calendar. All eight Ivy League universities \'97 Harvard, Yale, Princeton, Columbia, Brown, Dartmouth, Cornell, and the University of Pennsylvania \'97 released Regular Decision notifications for the Class of 2030 simultaneously. Acceptance rates are expected to remain in the 3\'967% range, consistent with recent cycles.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >Several structural features defined this cycle. Most Ivies reinstated standardized testing requirements for the Class of 2030 \'97 Harvard, Yale, Brown, Dartmouth, Penn, and Cornell all require scores again \'97 making the applicant pool more directly comparable and, in many analysts' assessment, more competitive. Princeton and Harvard continue to report the lowest acceptance rates, typically in the 3\'964% range. Despite predictions that political turbulence and visa uncertainty would reduce international applications, early data from several schools suggested international interest held steady or increased.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >Top public universities have become Ivy-level competitive this cycle. Georgia Tech set a record for in-state early applicants. UNC Chapel Hill's acceptance rate has fallen from 23% to 16% since 2019. Families whose students did not receive Ivy acceptance letters should understand clearly: the strength of public flagship and strong liberal arts college options has never been greater.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 elite-spotlight\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-label\cf4 ">
\f2 \uc0\u55356 \u57235 
\f0  Ivy Day 2026 \'97 What to Expect by Outcome</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-title\cf4 ">A Decision Guide for Every Result</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-items\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-item\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-school\cf4 ">Accepted</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-detail\cf4 ">Congratulations. You have until May 1 \'97 National College Decision Day \'97 to commit. Do not decide until you have compared financial aid packages carefully across every offer you've received. The most prestigious name is not always the best fit or the best value.</\cf5 div\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-item\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-school\cf4 ">Waitlisted</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-detail\cf4 ">A waitlist is not a denial. Opt in immediately if you want to be considered. Send a strong Letter of Continued Interest. Secure your spot at another school by May 1. Waitlist movement varies dramatically year to year and by institution.</\cf5 div\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-item\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-school\cf4 ">Denied</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-detail\cf4 ">A denial from an Ivy League school says nothing about your academic quality or future potential. It says you were one of tens of thousands of qualified applicants applying for very few seats. Your alternatives \'97 strong public flagships, liberal arts colleges, honors programs \'97 are excellent.</\cf5 div\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 spot-note\cf4 ">The May 1 deadline is firm. Do not commit to any school before comparing every financial aid package you have received. Ask schools whether they will match or improve competing offers. This is standard practice and you should not feel awkward asking.</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-box\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-label\cf4 ">Dr. Hurwitz's Take</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-text\cf4 ">Every year, families conflate the emotional weight of Ivy Day with the practical question of where to enroll. They are different questions. The acceptance decision is made by the institution. The enrollment decision is made by you \'97 and it should be based on fit, financial aid, program quality, and your student's specific goals, not on institutional prestige alone. Whatever the news tonight, the work of making a good college decision continues. That is where I can help.</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-cons\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">If Accepted</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Compare every financial aid package before committing \'97 Ivy aid is generally strong but not always the best available offer</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Attend admitted students' day and speak with current students, not just administrators</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Ask specifically about research opportunities for undergraduates given current NIH funding pressures</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 cons-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">If Waitlisted or Denied</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Do not make any enrollment decision emotionally \'97 give yourself 48 hours before acting</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Your alternatives are genuinely excellent \'97 strong public flagships admit more students to honors programs and research opportunities than ever</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >If waitlisted, Letter of Continued Interest should be specific and brief \'97 one page maximum</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

