\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf7 <!-- TOPIC 02: STUDENT LOANS \uc0\u8594  TREASURY -->
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 article\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-section\cf4 " id="\cf6 t02\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-number-row\cf4 "><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-num\cf4 ">TOPIC 02</\cf5 span\cf4 ><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-cat\cf4 ">Federal Student Aid / Policy</\cf5 span\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 h2\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-title\cf4 ">Trump Moves $1.7 Trillion Student Loan Portfolio to Treasury \'97 What It Means for 43 Million Borrowers</\cf5 h2\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-deck\cf4 ">The Trump administration announced a three-phase transfer of federal student loan management from the Department of Education to the Treasury Department. The first phase \'97 defaulted loans \'97 is already underway. This is the largest restructuring of student aid administration in the program's 40-year history.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-rule\cf4 "></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-grid\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-main\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-body\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >On March 19, 2026, the Trump administration announced the Federal Student Assistance Partnership \'97 a multi-phase agreement that will transfer the $1.7 trillion federal student loan portfolio from the Department of Education to the Department of the Treasury. In the first phase, already underway, Treasury assumes responsibility for collecting on defaulted loans \'97 approximately $180 billion affecting 9.2 million borrowers currently in default and an additional 2.4 million in late-stage delinquency.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >In subsequent phases, Treasury will assume operational responsibility for all non-defaulted loans and, eventually, administer the Free Application for Federal Student Aid (FAFSA). Education Secretary Linda McMahon called it a "hard reset," describing the Education Department as "never intended to operate what would be the fifth-largest commercial bank in the United States." Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent said the move would bring "long overdue financial discipline" to a portfolio he described as "badly mismanaged for years."</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >Critics, including the American Federation of Government Employees and student loan advocacy groups, called the transfer "irresponsible and reckless," noting that the Government Accountability Office has already warned that staffing reductions at Federal Student Aid have compromised oversight of loan servicers. For current borrowers, the administration says no action is required \'97 loans will remain with the same servicers and be repaid through the same channels. But the transition creates meaningful new uncertainty about repayment plan administration, income-driven repayment options, and future FAFSA administration.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-box\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-label\cf4 ">Dr. Hurwitz's Take</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-text\cf4 ">For families with students currently in school, the near-term impact is limited \'97 existing aid packages are not affected by this change. The concern is longer-term: as the Education Department is progressively dismantled, the policy expertise, institutional knowledge, and legal authority that protect borrowers' rights is dispersed across agencies without a history of student aid administration. Families currently navigating repayment should document everything, understand their rights under current law, and watch FAFSA administration carefully as Treasury takes over that function in future phases.</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-cons\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">Potential Benefits</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Treasury's financial management expertise may improve collections on defaulted loans and reduce waste</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Administration says borrowers will see no disruption \'97 same servicers, same repayment processes</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Some analysts see long-term efficiency gains from consolidating financial management under Treasury</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 cons-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">Key Concerns</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >GAO has warned that FSA staffing cuts have already compromised oversight of loan servicers who have been breaking the law</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Treasury has no history of administering student aid programs at scale \'97 a 2015 pilot with defaulted loans was limited</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Future FAFSA administration by Treasury creates uncertainty for families applying for aid in coming years</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
