\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 article\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-section\cf4 " id="\cf6 t03\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-number-row\cf4 "><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-num\cf4 ">TOPIC 03</\cf5 span\cf4 ><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-cat\cf4 ">Financial Aid / Pell Grant</\cf5 span\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 h2\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-title\cf4 ">The Pell Grant Is Running Out of Money: A $5.5B Shortfall and What It Means for College Access</\cf5 h2\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-deck\cf4 ">The Congressional Budget Office projects a $5.5 billion Pell Grant shortfall for FY 2026 alone, growing to $11.5 billion in FY 2027. Over 60 higher education organizations are urging Congress to act. Students who receive Pell Grants \'97 more than seven million of them \'97 need to understand what is at stake.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-rule\cf4 "></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-grid\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-main\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-body\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >The Pell Grant program \'97 the cornerstone of federal college access for low-income students, providing up to $6,335 annually to more than seven million recipients \'97 is facing a structural funding crisis. The Congressional Budget Office's February 2026 baseline projects a $5.5 billion shortfall for fiscal year 2026 and a cumulative gap of $104 billion over the next decade. Congress provided a one-time $10.5 billion injection last year, but it was insufficient to resolve the underlying structural mismatch between program costs and annual appropriations.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >The crisis is the product of two intersecting forces: the FAFSA Simplification Act of 2020, which expanded eligibility and brought 1.7 million additional students into Pell qualification, and the new Workforce Pell expansion taking effect July 1, 2026, which extends grants to short-term training programs. More students are receiving Pell Grants \'97 which is a policy success \'97 but Congress has not funded the expansion at the levels required to sustain it.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >Over 60 organizations \'97 including the NAACP, the American Federation of Teachers, and the Institute for College Access and Success \'97 sent a letter to congressional leadership urging $16.5 billion in emergency funding to cover both the 2026 and projected 2027 shortfalls. If Congress does not act before September 30, students could face eligibility restrictions or reduced awards beginning with the 2028\'962029 academic year. For families with students currently in school, this is a 2028 problem that requires 2026 action to prevent.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-box\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-label\cf4 ">Dr. Hurwitz's Take</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-text\cf4 ">This is the most significant financial aid story of 2026 for low- and middle-income families, and it is not receiving nearly enough attention. Seven million students depend on Pell Grants. The maximum award already covers the lowest share of college costs in the program's history. A funding cut would fall hardest on the students who are already stretching furthest to afford college. Families with current Pell recipients should monitor this closely. Families planning for college starting in 2028 and beyond should treat potential Pell reductions as a planning scenario to model now.</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-cons\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">Potential Protections</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Bipartisan support for Pell has historically been strong \'97 rural students in red states rely heavily on the program</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Many rural and red-state lawmakers will face political pressure not to cut a program their constituents depend on</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Coalition of 60+ organizations provides organized advocacy pressure on Congress</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 cons-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">Key Risks</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Without congressional action by September 30, award cuts could begin for the 2028\'962029 academic year</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >The 10-year cumulative shortfall of $104 billion is structural \'97 emergency fixes have not resolved the underlying problem</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Workforce Pell expansion launching July 1 adds further cost pressure before the structural shortfall is resolved</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
