\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-number-row\cf4 "><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-num\cf4 ">TOPIC 04</\cf5 span\cf4 ><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-cat\cf4 ">Research Funding / University Science</\cf5 span\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 h2\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-title\cf4 ">NIH Has Distributed Only 15% of Its Research Budget \'97 University Labs Are in Crisis</\cf5 h2\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-deck\cf4 ">The National Institutes of Health has obligated only $5.8 billion of its estimated $38 billion annual research budget at the fiscal midpoint \'97 15% of expected distribution. A nationwide survey of nearly 1,000 NIH-funded researchers describes lab closures, career exits, and what one UCLA scientist called "the Titanic hitting the iceberg."</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-rule\cf4 "></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-grid\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-main\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-body\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >At the halfway point of fiscal year 2026, the National Institutes of Health has obligated only $5.8 billion of an estimated $38 billion research budget \'97 approximately 15% of expected annual distribution, according to an analysis by the Association of American Medical Colleges released March 25, 2026. In prior years, NIH had typically distributed between $9 and $11 billion by this point in the fiscal year. The agency says it will spend its full annual budget before year-end, but scientists and research administrators warn that a year-end rush of grant awards creates its own cascading problems \'97 labs cannot hire, projects cannot launch, and early-career researchers cannot make career commitments on the basis of grants that may arrive in September and require spending within weeks.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >A nationwide STAT News survey of nearly 1,000 NIH-funded researchers, published March 19, 2026, found widespread disruption across the research enterprise. The UCLA Center for Behavioral and Addiction Medicine has shrunk by 40% due to funding cuts. The number of researchers receiving R01 grants \'97 the NIH's primary mechanism \'97 fell from 7,720 in 2024 to 5,885 in 2025. Funding rates for early-career investigators dropped from 26% to 19%. Early-career and female researchers were disproportionately affected: 60% of terminated grants led by doctoral students and assistant professors were led by women.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >For families with students at research universities, the NIH funding crisis has direct implications. The undergraduate research opportunities, faculty mentorship, and lab infrastructure that distinguish elite research universities depend fundamentally on sustained federal research investment. Schools that are cutting research staff and closing labs are not the same institutions they were two years ago.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-box\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-label\cf4 ">Dr. Hurwitz's Take</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-text\cf4 ">Families choosing between research universities should ask specific questions about NIH funding exposure, lab stability, and undergraduate research availability before committing. The reputation of a research university is built over decades; its research infrastructure can be damaged in a single year of funding disruption. A university with a stellar historical reputation in biomedical research may be operating very differently from what its rankings suggest. Ask directly, and ask recently.</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-cons\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">What Is Protected</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Congress rejected the administration's proposed 40% cut to NIH \'97 the budget is not being slashed, only delayed</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Courts have blocked the administration's attempt to cut indirect cost rates \'97 research infrastructure funding is legally protected for now</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >NIH has committed to spending its full annual budget before the fiscal year ends September 30</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 cons-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">What Is At Risk</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Early-career researchers and lab personnel cannot sustain employment during funding gaps \'97 labs are losing people who cannot wait for September grants</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Grants in DEI-adjacent, diversity, and international research areas have been specifically terminated \'97 some fields are effectively defunded</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Grant rates are falling even within the existing budget \'97 forward-funding practices reduce the number of new grants awarded annually</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

