\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 article\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-section\cf4 " id="\cf6 t05\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-number-row\cf4 "><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-num\cf4 ">TOPIC 05</\cf5 span\cf4 ><\cf5 span\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-cat\cf4 ">Enrollment Trends / College Value</\cf5 span\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 h2\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-title\cf4 ">The Enrollment Paradox: College Attendance Is Rising Even as Confidence in Higher Education Falls</\cf5 h2\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-deck\cf4 ">U.S. college enrollment reached 19.4 million students in fall 2025 \'97 above pre-pandemic levels for the third consecutive year \'97 even as public confidence in higher education has dropped to historic lows. New Chalkbeat analysis unpacks the paradox that should reframe how families think about the college decision.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-rule\cf4 "></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-grid\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 article-main\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 topic-body\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >A March 24, 2026 Chalkbeat analysis of National Student Clearinghouse data documents a striking paradox: overall college enrollment reached 19.4 million students in fall 2025, above pre-pandemic levels for the third consecutive year \'97 even as public confidence in higher education has fallen to historic lows and political attacks on universities have intensified. Community colleges saw the largest growth, at 3%, while public four-year institutions grew by 1.2%. Private four-year institutions bucked the trend, declining 1.4% \'97 the only sector that moved opposite public institutions in recent memory.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >The enrollment surge is driven by economic fundamentals that remain unchanged despite the political noise: college-educated workers continue to significantly out-earn those without degrees, and rising unemployment rates \'97 which make additional education more appealing \'97 have corresponded with the enrollment uptick. The negative public discourse about higher education is concentrated on elite institutions that educate a tiny fraction of American students and has not meaningfully deterred students from pursuing degrees at community colleges, regional publics, and strong four-year institutions.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 p\cf4 >At the same time, the longer-term demographic picture demands attention. The so-called enrollment cliff \'97 driven by declining birth rates after the 2008 recession \'97 begins its steepest phase in fall 2026, with the number of 18-year-olds projected to drop by 15% by 2029. This will not affect highly selective institutions, which will continue to have far more qualified applicants than seats. But it will significantly reshape the competitive landscape at smaller regional institutions, creating both closures and \'97 for families who choose strategically \'97 significant leverage in financial aid negotiations.</\cf5 p\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-box\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-label\cf4 ">Dr. Hurwitz's Take</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 take-text\cf4 ">The enrollment paradox reinforces a message I have shared for years: the narrative about higher education crisis is primarily a story about elite institutions, told by people paying attention to elite institutions. The reality for the vast majority of college students is more positive, more affordable, and more opportunity-rich than the headlines suggest. The enrollment cliff, meanwhile, creates real leverage for families who apply strategically to schools below the most selective tier \'97 those institutions need students, and their financial aid offices know it. Use this moment intelligently.</\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-cons\cf4 ">
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pros-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">Opportunities Created</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Enrollment cliff at regional institutions creates financial aid leverage for families who apply strategically</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Community college growth signals expanding dual enrollment and transfer pathways \'97 strong options for flexible planning</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >Public flagship institutions remain strong and increasingly competitive \'97 a UVA or UNC education has never been more valuable relative to cost</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 <\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 cons-card\cf4 "><\cf5 div\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-label\cf4 ">Risks to Watch</\cf5 div\cf4 ><\cf5 ul\cf4  class="\cf6 pc-list\cf4 "><\cf5 li\cf4 >Private four-year enrollment decline signals institutional financial stress \'97 research your target school's financial health</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >16 nonprofit colleges closed in 2025 \'97 small private institutions warrant scrutiny of financial stability before enrollment</\cf5 li\cf4 ><\cf5 li\cf4 >AI disruption of the job market may affect the long-term ROI calculation for certain degree programs</\cf5 li\cf4 ></\cf5 ul\cf4 ></\cf5 div\cf4 >
\f1\fs32 \cf3 \

\f0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf4 </\cf5 div\cf4 >
